'''utlegation†''' (ut-lē-gā′shọn), ''n.'' [For *''utlagation'', < ML. ''utlagatio''(''n''-), < ''ūtlagare'', outlaw: see ''outlaw'', ''v.''] The act of outlawing; outlawry. ''S. Butler'', Hudibras, III. i. 205.

'''utmost''' (ut′mōst), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''utmest'', ''utemest'', ''utemæste'', ''outemeste'', < AS. ''utemest'', ''ytmest'', ''ytemest'', < ''ūt'', out, + double superl. suffix -''m''-''est'': see ''out'' and -''most''. Cf. ''outmost'', a doublet of ''utmost''; cf. also ''uttermost''.] '''I.''' ''a. superl.'' '''1.''' Being at the furthest point or extremity or bound; furthest; extreme; last.
{{smaller block|Take you off his ''vtmost'' weed, and beholde the comelinesse, beautie, and riches which lie hid within his inward sense and sentence. ''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', To the Reader.}}
{{smaller block|Many wise men have miscarried in praising great designes before the ''utmost'' event.
{{right|''Milton'', Apology for Smectymnuus.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A white gull flew
Straight toward the ''utmost'' boundary of the East.</poem>
{{right|''R. W. Gilder'', New Day, Prelude.}}}}
'''2.''' Of the greatest or highest degree, number, quantity, or the like: as, the ''utmost'' assiduity; the ''utmost'' harmony; the ''utmost'' misery or happiness.
{{smaller block|<poem>I’ll . . . undertake to bring him
Where he shall answer, by a lawful form,
In peace, to his ''utmost'' peril.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iii. 1. 326.}}}}
{{smaller block|Many haue done their ''utmost'' best, sincerely and truly, according to their conceit, opinion, and vnderstanding.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' True Travels, II. 108.}}}}
{{smaller block|He showed the ''utmost'' aversion to business.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 2.}}}}

